{
  "comment": "NOT LOADED BY ANYTHING YET, which is why it sits in tools/extracted/ rather than in data/: that directory holds what the tool LOADS and DataFilesTests enforces it both ways. This is the vendored groundwork for reading the monster tables out of the install; it moves to data/ in the same change as the reader that opens it. Column order of the tables a monster is described by, from poe-tool-dev/dat-schema (PoE2 entries; its CI republishes schema.min.json on every commit). Offsets are NOT stored - they are arithmetic over the widths in DatColumns, and Mods is why that matters: its eight Stat*Value columns are INTERVALS, two values each, and pricing them as one put the whole table thirty-two bytes short. WHY THE INSTALL: measured against a live 0.5.5 client, the loader's file table names 153 tables and FIVE of these are not among them - MonsterVarieties, MonsterTypes, BloodTypes, Tags and GrantedEffects. Only MonsterResistances (22 rows of 520) and Mods (16784 of 693) are, and both agree with the sizes computed here, which is the one cross-check the client can give. The rest are checked against the FILE's own row size at runtime - LoadedTable.Agrees. THE JOIN, all of it inside MonsterVarieties: MonsterType -> MonsterTypes (-> MonsterResistances), BloodType -> BloodTypes, Tags -> Tags (whose DisplayString is the human-readable name the shipped export never carried), GrantedEffects -> GrantedEffects for the skills, and five separate mod arrays - Mods, Mods2, Special_Mods, Part1_Mods, Part2_Mods, Endgame_Mods - into Mods, whose Stat1..Stat8 reach Stats for the stat id and whose Stat*Value intervals carry the min and max. A stat id is what the install's .csd files turn into a sentence, which is what makes a monster mod readable rather than a number. Computed sizes: MonsterVarieties 1030 (0x406), MonsterTypes 50 (0x32), MonsterResistances 520 (0x208), BloodTypes 116 (0x74), Tags 44 (0x2C), GrantedEffects 297 (0x129), Mods 693 (0x2B5), Stats 106 (0x6A). ONE TYPE IS CORRECTED AGAINST THE DATA RATHER THAN COPIED: dat-schema calls PoiseThreshold an i32, and the values the export produced for it are 0.05, 0.055, 0.065, 0.08 and 0.25 - numbers no integer column can hold and no divisor turns a small set of integers into. It is an f32. The WIDTH is four bytes either way, so no row size moves; what moves is whether the column reads as 0.05 or as 1028443341, which is 0.05f's bit pattern. CONFIRMED AGAINST A LIVE 0.5.5 CLIENT on 2026-09-16, which is what the five install-only layouts had been waiting for - every one of the eight agreed with the file's own row size, which a .dat declares by where it puts its separator: MonsterVarieties 2793x1030, MonsterTypes 1571x50, MonsterResistances 22x520, BloodTypes 56x116, Tags 1339x44, GrantedEffects 8349x297, Mods 16784x693, Stats 27281x106. All eight live under data/balance/ with the .datc64 extension, and MonsterVarieties' arrays are CountFirst. THE STRONGER CHECK IS THE SECOND ROUTE: read this way the install holds 2792 monsters against the export's 2733 - 59 the export never knew about and NONE lost, so the install is a strict superset - and of the 2733 shared paths only 9 differ on name, type or skill count. Those nine are the export being out of date rather than either being wrong: Black Scythe Arbalist became Arbalest, Uxmal gained a skill. Two were this reader's own fault and are fixed - the game writes \"Gulzal, the Living Furnace \" with a trailing space and the text columns are trimmed now. The used subsets match exactly where they can: 185 tags of 1339 and 1214 types of 1571, the same counts the export carries.",
  "source": "poe-tool-dev/dat-schema, release latest, read 2026-09-16",
